pected-pakistani-drone-recovered-near-international-border-in-jks-samba/articleshow/135756854.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T11:02:14+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Abhishek Mishra</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135756924,imgsize-1552462.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[India simplifies visa rules: Categories cut to 9 from 13, application fields reduced by up to 70%]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/india-simplifies-visa-rules-categories-cut-to-9-from-13-application-fields-reduced-by-up-to-70/articleshow/135755163.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135755163.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[The Ministry of Home Affairs is revamping its visa system to improve transparency and efficiency. By significantly reducing the number of visa categories, the application process for foreign nationals becomes more straightforward. An e-visa option continues to be accessible for citizens from 172 countries, promoting travel to India. These adjustments will provide better predictability for medical treatments and extend student visa durations, supporting legitimate travel and business interactions.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/india-simplifies-visa-rules-categories-cut-to-9-from-13-application-fields-reduced-by-up-to-70/articleshow/135755163.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/india-simplifies-visa-rules-categories-cut-to-9-from-13-application-fields-reduced-by-up-to-70/articleshow/135755163.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T09:58:43+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Bharti Jain</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135755238,imgsize-121824.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Kulgam CRPF attack: Manhunt intensifies for LeT affiliated terrorist Md Lateef Bhat]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/kulgam-crpf-attack-manhunt-intensifies-for-wanted-terrorist-mohammad-lateef-bhat/articleshow/135755044.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135755044.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[Security agencies are intensifying their manhunt for local terrorist Mohammad Lateef Bhat in the Kulgam area. Suspected in a recent assault on CRPF personnel, Bhat has a notorious reputation for orchestrating Over Ground Worker networks. His connections to several terror incidents have alarmed officials. Previously, he dodged arrest during a confrontation with security forces in Shopian. Local authorities urge citizens to assist in tracking him and the other fugitives.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/kulgam-crpf-attack-manhunt-intensifies-for-wanted-terrorist-mohammad-lateef-bhat/articleshow/135755044.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/kulgam-crpf-attack-manhunt-intensifies-for-wanted-terrorist-mohammad-lateef-bhat/articleshow/135755044.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T09:51:20+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Raj Shekhar Jha</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135755108,imgsize-79710.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Bullet-riddled bodies of 2 missing police officers found in J&amp;K's Kishtwar]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/jk-bullet-riddled-bodies-of-2-missing-spos-found-in-kishtwar-weapons-missing/articleshow/135754703.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135754703.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/jk-bullet-riddled-bodies-of-2-missing-spos-found-in-kishtwar-weapons-missing/articleshow/135754703.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/jk-bullet-riddled-bodies-of-2-missing-spos-found-in-kishtwar-weapons-missing/articleshow/135754703.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T09:28:02+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Abhishek Mishra</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135755110,imgsize-1234326.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Watch: ‘Illegal’ tin shed removed at TMC MP Abhishek Banerjee's Kalighat residence]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/watch-illegal-tin-shed-removed-at-tmc-mp-abhishek-banerjees-kalighat-residence/articleshow/135753880.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135753880.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[Abhishek Banerjee raised concerns about ongoing surveillance by Kolkata Police near his residence. Following a previous attack during his birthday celebrations, he fears another incident may be planned. The temporary shed outside his house was removed by the municipal corporation on police orders. Despite multiple complaints, Banerjee accused the BJP government of failing to take action against the situation.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/watch-illegal-tin-shed-removed-at-tmc-mp-abhishek-banerjees-kalighat-residence/articleshow/135753880.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/watch-illegal-tin-shed-removed-at-tmc-mp-abhishek-banerjees-kalighat-residence/articleshow/135753880.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T09:22:28+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Pranjal Pandey</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135754494,imgsize-93950.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[New blue-and-white droplet logo on milk, curd and paneer packs from July 2028]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/new-blue-and-white-droplet-logo-on-milk-curd-and-paneer-packs-from-july-2028/articleshow/135753118.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135753118.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[FSSAI says it will help consumers identify dairy products]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/new-blue-and-white-droplet-logo-on-milk-curd-and-paneer-packs-from-july-2028/articleshow/135753118.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/new-blue-and-white-droplet-logo-on-milk-curd-and-paneer-packs-from-july-2028/articleshow/135753118.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T08:40:15+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Anuja Jaiswal</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135753181,imgsize-174242.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[India slips 5 spots on climate change performance index]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/india-slips-5-spots-on-climate-change-index/articleshow/135753099.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135753099.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[India has experienced a decline in the climate change performance index, falling from prior high rankings. The country now sits at 28th position due to rising greenhouse gas emissions and energy use. While India has made significant strides in renewable energy, the overall performance remains low. Comparatively, Pakistan, with lower emissions, is ranked just above India at 26th.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/india-slips-5-spots-on-climate-change-index/articleshow/135753099.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/india-slips-5-spots-on-climate-change-index/articleshow/135753099.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T08:34:33+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Vishwa Mohan</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135753289,imgsize-400824.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Pollution's hidden health cost: Dirty air may also raise diabetes risk, global review finds]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/pollutions-hidden-health-cost-dirty-air-may-also-raise-diabetes-risk-global-review-finds/articleshow/135753090.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135753090.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[Recent findings highlight a concerning connection between PM2.5 air pollution and an increased risk of type 2 diabetes, showing that every 10 microgram per cubic meter rise in PM2.5 could boost diabetes risk by 10% to 16%. Notably, South Asian communities, particularly in India and Bangladesh, may face a 9% heightened risk. This underscores the urgency for improved air quality management to mitigate metabolic diseases, although definitive causation remains unestablished.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/pollutions-hidden-health-cost-dirty-air-may-also-raise-diabetes-risk-global-review-finds/articleshow/135753090.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/pollutions-hidden-health-cost-dirty-air-may-also-raise-diabetes-risk-global-review-finds/articleshow/135753090.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T08:28:14+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Anuja Jaiswal</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135753126,imgsize-57960.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Centre looks to open up car-rental market, cuts proposed minimum fleet to five]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/centre-looks-to-open-up-car-rental-market-cuts-proposed-minimum-fleet-to-five/articleshow/135751957.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135751957.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/centre-looks-to-open-up-car-rental-market-cuts-proposed-minimum-fleet-to-five/articleshow/135751957.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/centre-looks-to-open-up-car-rental-market-cuts-proposed-minimum-fleet-to-five/articleshow/135751957.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T07:03:16+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Dipak K Dash</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135751991,imgsize-111682.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Navi Mumbai India's cleanest city, Indore's domination ends]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/navi-mumbai-indias-cleanest-city-indores-domination-ends/articleshow/135748695.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748695.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[For the 2025-26 Swachh Survekshan Awards, Navi Mumbai has claimed the esteemed title of the cleanest city. Following closely are Surat and Vijayawada, securing the second and third positions, respectively. Additionally, Rewa from Madhya Pradesh has been honored as the cleanest riverfront town this year. Significant mentions include Rudrapur as the cleanest Himalayan city, along with various award categories highlighting different city populations.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/navi-mumbai-indias-cleanest-city-indores-domination-ends/articleshow/135748695.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/navi-mumbai-indias-cleanest-city-indores-domination-ends/articleshow/135748695.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T02:55:54+05:30</pubDate><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748773,imgsize-104242.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Collegium wants transfer of HC judges streamlined, random ways discarded]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/collegium-wants-transfer-of-hc-judges-streamlined-random-ways-discarded/articleshow/135748630.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748630.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[The Supreme Court collegium is working to establish a systematic policy for transferring judges among high courts. Currently, transfers are largely subject to personal criteria, which the collegium aims to revise. This new policy emphasizes objective reasoning to streamline the transfer process, reduce personal biases, and enhance accountability regarding judges’ conduct and expertise, marking a pivotal evolution in judicial integrity management.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/collegium-wants-transfer-of-hc-judges-streamlined-random-ways-discarded/articleshow/135748630.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/collegium-wants-transfer-of-hc-judges-streamlined-random-ways-discarded/articleshow/135748630.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T02:44:43+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Dhananjay Mahapatra</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748659,imgsize-124410.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[SC: Indians married here can't get divorced abroad]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/sc-indians-married-here-cant-get-divorced-abroad/articleshow/135748558.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748558.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[In a landmark decision, the Supreme Court declared that divorces executed overseas, based on grounds not acknowledged under Indian law, are invalid. This ruling implies that individuals who remarry after such foreign divorces may risk prosecution for bigamy. The court also affirmed that marriages conducted according to Indian traditions hold validity, irrespective of any foreign citizenship.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/sc-indians-married-here-cant-get-divorced-abroad/articleshow/135748558.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/sc-indians-married-here-cant-get-divorced-abroad/articleshow/135748558.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T02:38:33+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Amit Anand Choudhary</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748598,imgsize-95716.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Complete data could've raised average Diwali pollution level in Delhi by 49%]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/complete-data-couldve-raised-average-diwali-pollution-level-49/articleshow/135748336.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748336.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[On Diwali night, the absence of PM2.5 data from monitoring stations led to distorted pollution readings in Delhi. Research by the Centre for Research on Energy and Clean Air indicated that pollution levels might have increased by as much as 49%. While the Central Pollution Control Board pointed out calibration limitations, readings from the Delhi Pollution Control Committee were inconsistent.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/complete-data-couldve-raised-average-diwali-pollution-level-49/articleshow/135748336.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/complete-data-couldve-raised-average-diwali-pollution-level-49/articleshow/135748336.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T02:03:33+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Priyangi Agarwal</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748346,imgsize-49840.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Yuge Yugeen museum set to open doors to public next year; cabinet approves Rs 3,230 cr]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/yuge-yugeen-museum-set-to-open-doors-to-public-next-year-cabinet-approves-rs-3230-cr/articleshow/135748181.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748181.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/yuge-yugeen-museum-set-to-open-doors-to-public-next-year-cabinet-approves-rs-3230-cr/articleshow/135748181.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/yuge-yugeen-museum-set-to-open-doors-to-public-next-year-cabinet-approves-rs-3230-cr/articleshow/135748181.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T01:22:08+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Dipak K Dash and Ambika Pandit</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748186,imgsize-71722.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[NTA plans exam centres in every district]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/nta-plans-exam-centres-in-every-district/articleshow/135748060.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesof